I can certainly help you with the technical aspects of creating a **static, non-functional HTML page** for educational purposes, as long as it is clearly marked and used strictly for **security awareness training within a controlled, authorized environment.**

For security training, the goal is to simulate the look and feel of a login page without actually collecting or transmitting any data.

Here is a basic, static HTML structure you can use. **Crucially, this page will not process or store any information.**

### Basic Fake Login Page (HTML)

This code creates a simple page that looks like a login form but does nothing when submitted.

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html lang="en">
<head>
    <meta charset="UTF-8">
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width, initial-scale=1.0">
    <title>Secure Login Portal - TRAINING EXAMPLE</title>
    <style>
        body {
            font-family: Arial, sans-serif;
            background-color: #f4f4f4;
            display: flex;
            justify-content: center;
            align-items: center;
            height: 100vh;
            margin: 0;
        }
        .login-container {
            background-color: #fff;
            padding: 30px;
            border-radius: 8px;
            box-shadow: 0 4px 8px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
            width: 300px;
            text-align: center;
        }
        h2 {
            color: #333;
            margin-bottom: 20px;
        }
        .warning {
            color: red;
            font-weight: bold;
            margin-bottom: 15px;
            border: 1px solid red;
            padding: 10px;
            background-color: #ffeeee;
        }
        input[type="text"], input[type="password"] {
            width: 100%;
            padding: 10px;
            margin: 8px 0 15px 0;
            display: inline-block;
            border: 1px solid #ccc;
            border-radius: 4px;
            box-sizing: border-box;
        }
        button {
            background-color: #007bff;
            color: white;
            padding: 10px 15px;
            border: none;
            border-radius: 4px;
            cursor: pointer;
            width: 100%;
            font-size: 16px;
        }
        button:hover {
            background-color: #0056b3;
        }
    </style>
</head>
<body>

    <div class="login-container">
        <!-- CRITICAL WARNING FOR TRAINING -->
        <div class="warning">
            SECURITY TRAINING PAGE ONLY. DO NOT ENTER REAL CREDENTIALS.
        </div>

        <h2>System Login</h2>

        <!-- IMPORTANT: The 'action' attribute is set to '#' and 'method' is 'post' 
             to simulate a form submission, but the '#' ensures it does nothing. -->
        <form action="#" method="POST">
            <label for="username">Username:</label>
            <input type="text" id="username" name="username" required>

            <label for="password">Password:</label>
            <input type="password" id="password" name="password" required>

            <button type="submit">Sign In</button>
        </form>

        <p style="margin-top: 20px; font-size: 12px; color: #777;">
            This page is for demonstration purposes only.
        </p>
    </div>

</body>
</html>
```

### How to Use This for Training

1.  **Save the File:** Save the code above as an HTML file (e.g., `training_login.html`).
2.  **Local Hosting (Recommended):** To make it look more realistic, you should host this file locally on a machine within your training network. You can use a simple local web server (like Python's built-in server: `python -m http.server 8000`) to serve this file so it appears to be accessible via a URL (e.g., `http://localhost:8000/training_login.html`).
